A recurrence relation is a mathematical equation that describes a sequence of values, each of which is a function of the preceding values. In the context of algorithmic scrutiny, recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of algorithms, particularly those that use recursion.

The time complexity of an algorithm is a measure of the amount of computational time required to run the algorithm. This is often expressed in terms of Big O notation, which describes the upper bound of the time complexity in the worst-case scenario.

Recurrence relations are used to analyze the time complexity of recursive algorithms. For example, consider the following recursive algorithm for computing the factorial of a number:

```
function factorial(n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm can be described using a recurrence relation. For each recursive call to `factorial(n)`, the function makes one recursive call to `factorial(n - 1)`. Therefore, the time complexity T(n) of the algorithm can be described by the recurrence relation:

```
T(n) = T(n - 1) + O(1)
```

